.\"
'\" Tcl/Tk Macro definitions - borrowed from Tcl/Tk man pages.
'\"     # Set up traps and other miscellaneous stuff for Tcl/Tk man pages.
.if t .wh -1.3i ^B
.nr ^l \n(.l
.ad b
'\" .BS Start box enclosure.  From here until next .BE, everything will be
'\"     enclosed in one large box.
.de BS
.br
.mk ^y
.nr ^b 1u
.if n .nf
.if n .ti 0
.if n \l'\\n(.lu\(ul'
.if n .fi
..
./"
'\" .BE End of box enclosure.
.de BE
.nf
.ti 0
.mk ^t
.ie n \l'\\n(^lu\(ul'
.el \{\
.\"     Draw four-sided box normally, but don't draw top of
.\"     box if the box started on an earlier page.
.ie !\\n(^b-1 \{\
\h'-1.5n'\L'|\\n(^yu-1v'\l'\\n(^lu+3n\(ul'\L'\\n(^tu+1v-\\n(^yu'\l'|0u-1.5n\(ul'
.\}
.el \}\
\h'-1.5n'\L'|\\n(^yu-1v'\h'\\n(^lu+3n'\L'\\n(^tu+1v-\\n(^yu'\l'|0u-1.5n\(ul'
.\}
.\}
.fi
.br
.nr ^b 0
..
./"
'\" .CS Begin code excerpt.
.de CS
.RS
.nf
.ta .25i .5i .75i 1i
..
./"
'\" .CE End code excerpt.
.de CE
.fi
.RE
..
./"
'\" .DS Begin an indented unfilled display.
.de DS
.RS
.nf
.sp
..
./"
'\" .DE End of indented unfilled display.
'\"
.de DE
.fi
.RE
.sp
..
./"
'\" .OP cmdName dbName dbClass
'\"     Start of description of a specific option.  cmdName gives the
'\"     option's name as specified in the class command, dbName gives
'\"     the option's name in the option database, and dbClass gives
'\"     the option's class in the option database.
.de OP
.LP
.nf
.ta 4c
Command-Line Name:      \\fB\\$1\\fR
Database Name:  \\fB\\$2\\fR
Database Class: \\fB\\$3\\fR
.fi
.IP
..
./"
'\" .SO Start of list of standard options for a Tk widget.  The
'\"     options follow on successive lines, in four columns separated
'\"     by tabs.
.de SO
.SH "STANDARD OPTIONS"
.LP
.nf
.ta 4c 8c 12c
.ft B
..
./"
'\" .SE End of list of standard options for a Tk widget.
'\"     Start of description of a specific option.  cmdName gives the
'\"     option's name as specified in the class command, dbName gives
'\"     the option's name in the option database, and dbClass gives
'\"     the option's class in the option database.
.de SE
.fi
.ft R
.LP
See the \\fBoptions\\fR manual entry for details on the standard options.
..
.\"**********************************************************************
.\"				End of Macros!!!
.\"**********************************************************************
.TH CStatusBox n 03/08/98 "CADC"
.\".BS
.SH NAME
statusBox 
\- Create and manipulate statusBox widgets

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBstatusBox\fI \fIpathName \fR?\fIoptions\fR?

.SH "INHERITANCE"
itk::Widget <- LabeledWidget <- CStatusBox

.SO
\-background	\-foreground	\-relief
\-borderwidth	\-height	\-width		
\-cursor
.SE

.SH "INHERITED OPTIONS"
.LP
.nf
.ta 4c 8c 12c
\fB-clientdata\fR	\fB-labelimage\fR	\fB-labeltext\fR	
\fB-labelbitmap\fR	\fB-labelmargin\fR	\fB-labelvariable\fR
\fB-labelfont\fR	\fB-labelpos\fR	
.fi
.LP
See the "labeledwidget" class manual entry for details on the 
inherited options.

.SH "WIDGET-SPECIFIC OPTIONS"

.OP \-height height Height
Specifies the height of the status box childsite in any of the
forms acceptable to Tk_GetPixels. The default value is 0.

.OP \-statusbackground statusBackground Background
Specifies the normal background color of all the status items.

.OP \-statusforeground statusForeground Foreground
vSpecifies the normal foreground color of all the status items.

.OP \-statusmargin statusMargin Margin
Specifies the margin between the label and the childsite for all
of the status items.  See the "labeledwidget" \fI-labelmargin\fR
for more information.

.OP \-statuspos statusPos Position
Specifies the relative position of the label with respect to its
childsite for all of the status items. See the "labeledwidget" 
\fI-labelpos\fR for more information.

.OP \-width width Width
Specifies the desired width of the status box childsite in any of
the forms acceptable to Tk_GetPixels.  The default value is 0.
.\".BE

.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The \fBCStatusBox\fR command creates a new window (given by the
\fIpathName\fR argument) and makes it into a CStatusBox widget.
Additional options, described above, may be specified on the 
command line or in the option database to configure aspects of
the status box. The \fBCStatusBox\fR command returns its
\fIpathName\fR argument.  At the time this command is invoked,
there must not exist a window named \fIpathName\fR, but
\fIpathName\fR's parent must exist.
.PP
The \fBCStatusBox\fR command creates a box widget 
capable of adding, inserting, deleting, and configuring 
widgets derived from the labeledwidget class, these widgets
are referred to as the status items.

.SH "WINDGET COMMAND"
The \fBCStatusBox\fR command creates a new Tcl command whose
name is \fIpathName\fR.  This
command may be used to invoke various
operations on the widget.  It has the following general form:
.CS
\fIpathName method \fR?\fIarg arg ...\fR?
.CE
\fIMethod\fR and the \fIarg\fRs
determine the exact behavior of the command.  
.PP
Many of the widget methods (commands) for the \fBCStatusBox\fR take an 
indicator as an argument.  The indicator informs the status box method which
status button to operate on.  These indicators
are called \fIindexes\fR and allow reference and manipulation of status buttons.
\fBCStatusBox\fR indexes may be specified in any of the following forms:
.TP 12
\fInumber\fR
Specifies the status button numerically, where 0 corresponds to the top 
status item of the status box.
.TP 12
\fIend\fR
Indicates the last status item  of the status box.
.TP 12
\fIpattern\fR
If the index does not satisfy one of the above forms then this
form is used.  \fIPattern\fR is pattern-matched against the tag of
each status item in the status box, in order from top to bottom,
until a matching entry is found.  The rules of \fBTcl_StringMatch\fR
are used.  The tag is the value given to the \fItag\fR argument
in either the \fBadd\fR or \fBinsert\fR method.

.PP
The following methods are possible for CStatusBox widgets:
.TP
\fIpathName \fBadd\fR \fIwidgetType\fR \fItag\fR ?\fIoption value option value\fR?
Adds a new status item, which is a "widgetType" widget to the status box 
window on the bottom.  The command
takes additional options which are passed on to the status item as construction
arguments.  These include the standard options for "widgetType".
The tag is returned.

.TP 
\fIpathName \fBalign\fR
Causes labels of the status items to be
aligned.  See the \fBlabeledwidget\fR class manual entry for more details
on the \fILabeledwidget::alignlabels\fR method.


.TP
\fIpathName \fBcget\fR \fIoption\fR
Returns the current value of the configuration option given
by \fIoption\fR.
\fIOption\fR may have any of the values accepted by the \fBCStatusBox\fR
command.

.TP
\fIpathName \fBchildsite\fR
Return the path name of the child site. All of the status items are
placed in the child site.

.TP
\fIpathName \fBcomponent\fR ?\fIname\fR? ?\fIcommand arg arg ...\fR?
Used to query or access component widgets within the \fBCStatusBox\fR
vwidget.  For example each of the status items is a component of
the \fBCStatusBox\fR widget.  With no arguments, this returns a list of
symbolic names for component widgets that are accessible in the
current scope.  
.sp
If a symbolic \fIname\fR is specified this method returns the window
path name for that component.
.sp
Otherwise, the \fIcommand\fR and any remaining \fIarg\fR arguments
are invoked as a method on the component with the symbolic \fIname\fR.
This provides a well-defined mechanism for accessing internal components
without relying on specific window path names.

.TP
\fIpathName \fBconfig\fR
See \fBconfigure\fI.

.TP
\fIpathName\fR \fBconfigure\fR ?\fIoption\fR? ?\fIvalue option value ...\fR?
Query or modify the configuration options of the widget.
If no \fIoption\fR is specified, returns a list describing all of
the available options for \fIpathName\fR (see \fBTk_ConfigureInfo\fR for
information on the format of this list).  If \fIoption\fR is specified
with no \fIvalue\fR, then the command returns a list describing the
one named option (this list will be identical to the corresponding
sublist of the value returned if no \fIoption\fR is specified).  If
one or more \fIoption\-value\fR pairs are specified, then the command
modifies the given widget option(s) to have the given value(s);  in
this case the command returns an empty string.
\fIOption\fR may have any of the values accepted by the \fBCStatusBox\fR
command.

.TP
\fIpathName \fBdelete\fR \fIindex\fR
Deletes (removes) a specified status item given an \fIindex\fR.

.TP
\fIpathName \fBindex\fR \fIindex\fR
Returns the numerical index corresponding to index.

.TP
\fIpathName \fBinsert \fIindex\fR \fIwidgetType\fR \fItag\fR ?\fIoption value option value ...\fR?
Same as the \fBadd\fR command except that it inserts the new
status item just before the one given by \fIindex\fR, instead of appending
to the end of the status box.  The \fIoption\fR, and \fIvalue\fR
arguments have the same interpretation as for the \fBadd\fR widget
command.  

.TP
\fIpathName \fBisa\fR \fIclassName\fR
Sets the status box's class to the specified class name for.

.TP
\fIpathName \fBitemConfigure\fR \fItag\fR ?\fIoption value option value ...\fR?
This command is similar to the \fBconfigure\fR widget command except
that it modifies options associated with status item given by \fItag\fR
instead of modifying options for the overall status box widget.   If no
\fIoption\fR is specified, the command returns a list describing all 
available options for \fItag\fR (see \fBTK_configureInfo\fR for information
on the format of the list).  If \fIoption\fR is specified with no value,
then the command returns a list describing the one named option.  If one 
or more \fIoption\-value\fR pairs are specified, then the command modifies
the given option(s) to have the given value(s) for the status item given
by  \fItag\fR.

.SH "COMPONENTS"
The only additional components are those that are create by
the \fBadd\fR and \fBinsert\fR methods described above.  A
new component is construstructed for each call to these methods.
The name of the component is the name specified by the
\fItag\fR argument.

.SH "DEFAULT BINDINGS"
.PP
No bindings are defined for this widget except those defined by
the inherited class.
.PP
See the \fBlabeledwidget\fR manual entry
for more information.
.PP
All of the status items added to the box have the same 
bindings as those specified
by the "widgetType" widget, given to the \fIadd\fR or \fIinsert\fR
methods.

.SH EXAMPLE
.DS
 CStatusBox .s -height 200 -width 100 -borderwidth 2 -relief ridge \\
     -labeltext "test"
 .s add entryfield item1 -labeltext "item 1:" -textvariable myVar
 .s add labeledwidget item2 -labeltext "label widget:"
 .s add optionmenu item5 -items "NONE MIN FULL" 		\\
     -labeltext "Debug Level:"
pack .s -fill both -expand y
.DE

.SH BUGS
Not all functionality is implement yet. In particular the \fIdelete\fR and
\fIinsert\fR widget commands.

.SH AUTHOR
Shannon Jaeger

.SH KEYWORDS
statusBox, widget
